#
# Security tier: locked
#
# A kiosk or an appliance: one program on the screen or behind a port, and a
# machine that should do nothing else. Every tool and builtin is off unless
# it is named here, STRICT=2, and the monitor is gone. See sec_default for
# the tiers and how to pick one.
#
#   sh build.sh debug_none limbo desktop serial sec_locked
#
# The allow list is what a boot script and a service supervisor use: move,
# copy, read and search files, look at the processes, the mounts and the
# kernel log, log a line, take a lock, time a command out, check a download's
# sha256, and power off. What it leaves out, it leaves out because each is a
# way out of a single-purpose machine:
#
#   the network        ip, host, fetch, wget
#   archives, codecs   tar, gzip, xz, zstd and their readers
#   languages          awk (sed stays; boot scripts edit a line with it)
#   privilege          chroot, chown, chgrp, nsenter, unshare, setpriv, chrt,
#                      taskset, prlimit, setarch, script, pivot_root
#   disks              mkswap, wipefs, blockdev, the partition editors, dd,
#                      shred, mknod
#   other users        wall, write, mesg, who, users, last
#   bowls and editing  bowl, edit
#   interactive shell  history, fc, bind, complete, the job control builtins,
#                      pushd and popd, alias, help, enable, builtin, source,
#                      declare, let, mapfile, shopt
#
# /init, /term and /moonwater stay whatever this says: the kernel and init
# run them by path. POSIX's special builtins with cd, true and false have no
# switch either; the shell is not a shell without them.
#

CONFIG_SECURITY=y
CONFIG_SECURITY_YAMA=y
CONFIG_INTEGRITY=n

CONFIG_MOONWATER_STRICT=2
CONFIG_MOONWATER_SHELL_MONITOR=n
CONFIG_MOONWATER_TOOLS_ALL=n
